messages:
Alex W.: hey team, saw an escalation from Acme Mobile — multiple users report the SSE stream disconnects mid-response when switching networks (4G -> wifi). they get partial output then the client stops receiving events.
Maria S.: oof. any error codes on the client side?
Alex W.: client logs show: `NET::ERR_INCOMPLETE_CHUNKED_ENCODING` on Chrome Android and `The network connection was lost` on iOS Safari. app-level logs show no explicit reconnect attempt (they rely on EventSource auto-reconnect). :frowning:
Jordan (ops): server side we see a surge of connections dropping with TCP FIN/RESET around the same timestamps. nginx access logs show status 499 for those requests (client closed). sample access line: `GET /v1/stream 499 - 10.1.7.45 - 01/Mar/2026:12:04:02 +0000 "-" "EventSource/1.0"`.
Alex W.: they reproduce reliably by starting a stream then toggling wifi. steps I have:
1) Start mobile Chrome on network A (cellular)
2) Open SSE stream to /v1/stream?session_id=abc
3) After ~7–15s toggle to wifi
4) Client shows partial data then connection dies, EventSource sometimes doesn't reconnect automatically.
Sam P.: does the client send `last-event-id` on reconnect attempt? that helps resume.
Alex W.: not in their current repro; their app doesn't persist last-event-id. also discovered Chrome collapses background sockets on some OEMs.
Maria S.: we've seen CDNs close idle connections after 60s. but this is within 10–20s which feels like local stack/TLS renegotiation fails during the link handoff.
edge-bot: noticed: upstream idle timeout 90s, keepalive timeout 75s on edge. (automated note)
Jordan (ops): logs show TLS handshake failures during the exact timeframe: `tls: received unexpected message` and `connection reset by peer`. likely the client TCP/TLS socket reset mid-handshake when switching interface.
Sam P.: reproduce locally with Chrome devtools network emulation? maybe emulate a flaky network and do a quick interface drop.
Alex W.: I repro'd on an Android Pixel: started an SSE stream, switched to airplane mode then re-enabled wifi — immediate partial data, then `EventSource` error event fires and sometimes auto-reconnect never happens (depends on timing).
Maria S.: suggested mitigations for customers:
- implement server heartbeat comments in SSE (send `: keepalive\n\n` every 10s) so network middleboxes don't treat stream as idle
- client should persist `Last-Event-ID` and explicitly reconnect on error with that header
- on mobile, call `eventSource.close()` then create a new EventSource after a short backoff rather than relying on implicit reconnect
- in our SDK, expose a reconnect policy and resume token API
Alex W.: sent these to Acme Mobile. they asked for short-term config changes on our side (edge/CDN) to be less aggressive. what can we do server-side?
Jordan (ops): options:
- increase edge keepalive to 120s and tweak TCP FIN timeouts (may help some carriers)
- add explicit tiny chunk flushes every 3–5s so some networks don't tear socket on interface change
- mark framing to encourage client re-open with a resume token (would need small protocol change)
Sam P.: infra note: Chrome's EventSource reconnect logic will only re-open if network stack reports socket close cleanly; if the TCP stack is in an intermediate state it can hang. forcing client to do an explicit restart is more reliable.
Maria S.: action items:
- support: get concrete repro devices/OS versions from customer + capture client console logs and `Last-Event-ID` behavior
- eng-runtime: quick patch to emit SSE comment keepalive and send a `\n` flushable comment every 5s for streaming routes
- edge: evaluate increasing keepalive/idle timeout for streaming endpoints behind CDN
- devex/sdk: add example snippet showing how to persist last-event-id and handle `onerror` to re-open explicitly
Alex W.: also flagged a bug where our proxy returns 502 if backend closes during TLS renegotiation. that makes an ugly failure mode instead of 499. Jordan, can you correlate 502 spikes?
Jordan (ops): will check historical metrics 12:00–13:00 UTC. initial look shows 499s spike coincident with customer report; small bump in 502s too. I'll dump request-ids and route to the thread.
Sam P.: workaround code snippet I suggested to Acme Mobile (they use JS):
```
function connect(session) {
  const es = new EventSource(`/v1/stream?session=${session}`);
  es.onmessage = e => {
    // persist last id
    localStorage.setItem('last_event', e.lastEventId);
    handleChunk(e.data);
  };
  es.onerror = () => {
    // ensure we fully close then backoff then re-create
    try { es.close(); } catch(e){}
    setTimeout(() => {
      const last = localStorage.getItem('last_event');
      const url = `/v1/stream?session=${session}`;
      const h = last ? { 'Last-Event-ID': last } : {};
      // use fetch + keepalive to trigger a warm handshake if needed
      fetch(url, { method: 'GET', keepalive: true, headers: h }).then(()=> connect(session));
    }, 1500);
  };
}
```
Alex W.: customer also requested a reproducible curl example for their network team. provided this:
```
curl -N -H "Accept: text/event-stream" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer <redacted>" \
  https://api.redwood.example/v1/stream?session=abc
# while running, toggle network interface on the device acting as client
```
Maria S.: next steps: Jordan to attach the request-ids + access/log snippets; eng-runtime to prototype keepalive comment change (low-risk); devex to draft SDK reconnection doc; support to coordinate with customer for device list and timeline.
Jordan (ops): attaching first batch of request-ids (copied below) for 12:03–12:07 UTC: `rid=rx1, rx2, rx3` — will drop full logs on the incident share.
Alex W.: thanks everyone. flagged as P2 escalation for now. I'll follow up once Acme tries SDK changes. :thumbsup:
